I pushed `feat/3-contracts` to `origin`. It's 4 signed commits; ruff, mypy and pytest pass (81 tests).

Before pushing I checked #3 against the other planned work (#10, #11, #12, and the #13 and #14 drafts):

- **No new fields needed now.** What they'll need can all be added later as optional fields without bumping `schema_version`. That covers how #10 identifies cases submitted by outside systems and its async job records, #12's per-draft log of agent steps, and #13's pending sensitivity tags.
- **One gap, now fixed.** The exported schemas reject unknown fields. Once #10 and #11 publish them, an outside client that checks our responses strictly would have broken on any added field. The new commit (`7b53f61`) spells out which changes need a version bump and which don't. It also says clients must ignore unknown fields and accept unknown enum values. Code and tests didn't change.

#13 and #14 are still committed locally in their own worktrees and branches, not pushed. I assigned both issues to you earlier; I can remove that while they're on hold.

**Shall I open the PR for #3?** It would close #3 and say what changed, why, and how it was tested. It would also ask the reviewer to look at the choices I made that you may want to change:
- the `Case` name instead of `Thread`;
- the `{{slot_name}}` placeholder syntax;
- the two safety rules the models enforce (only `low`-risk templates may auto-send, and no auto-send with a failed check);
- the fields I added beyond the issue (`Draft.id`, `message_seq`, and `model`/`created_at` on analyses);
- the format of the synthetic test files.

